oss 16 states and the District of Columbia. Mid-America Apartment Communities, Inc. was incorporated in 1977 in Tennessee and is based in Germantown, Tennessee.","ceo_bio":"Mr. Adrian Bradley Hill C.F.A. | CEO, President & Director | Age 49 | Compensation: $2.0M (FY2025)","culture_narrative":"Employs approximately 2K people. Revenue per employee of $881K ranks in the below the sector median among Real Estate peers, with revenue growing 12%/yr across its filed history. Each employee generates $286K of free cash flow annually. Stock-based compensation of $7K per employee reflects an ownership culture. CEO compensation is 2\u00d7 revenue per employee (modest relative to workforce productivity). Governance score flags low compensation governance risk (1/10).","financial_summary":["Mid-America Apartment Communiti is a large-cap real estate business with a $14.0B market cap on $2.2B of annual revenue.","The top line has compounded at +3% over the past three years, which puts it in mature, slow-growth territory.","Returns on capital run at 12% against a 8% cost of capital, a 4-point spread that creates value, though narrow enough that competitive pressure could close it.","The business throws off $718M in free cash flow \u2014 roughly 32 cents on every revenue dollar, which is the owner-earnings figure that actually belongs to shareholders, and cash conversion sits at 2.41\u00d7 so those profits are backed by real cash.","On the balance sheet, the balance sheet carries net cash, which is the gold standard: the business never has to make desperate decisions in a bad year, and interest coverage runs at 3.5\u00d7.","Management returns a meaningful 5.2% of market cap to owners through dividends and buybacks, a strong signal of disciplined capital allocation, and insider ownership is negligible, so management has little personal stake in the long-term outcome."],"sector_headwinds":[{"text":"Altman Z in the distress zone (1.7) \u2014 the kind of balance-sheet risk that can make 